PROPOSAL -- NOT APPLIED, NOT COMMITTED AS CODE. 2026-09-12, T26 lane.
Supersedes this lane's earlier conclusion that an AXIAL SPLIT of `env_wall` was
required.  The supervisor's hypothesis was tested against the geometry and is
correct; the axial split is NOT needed and is withdrawn.

THE MEASUREMENT THAT SETTLES IT -- read from the staged STLs, not inferred:

  duct.stl        768 triangles   x [0.000000, 0.200000]   r [0.125000, 0.130000]
                  1152 vertices EXACTLY at r = 0.125000
                  1152 vertices EXACTLY at r = 0.130000
  fluid_env.stl   768 triangles   x [-0.175000, 0.900000]  r [0.000000, 0.125000]
                  2112 vertices EXACTLY at r = 0.125000  (192 at r = 0, cap centres)

The duct IS a closed annular shell and its INNER BORE AT r = 0.125000 IS PRESENT
AS GEOMETRY over the whole span x in [0, 0.200].  `refinementSurfaces` already
gives `duct` `level (4 4)`, so that interface is refined to 1.1250 mm BY THE
DUCT ENTRY.  Over that span `fluid_env`'s wall is COINCIDENT with it -- same
radius, same 96-gon, and the builder already REFUSES unless the duct bore and
the hub share one phase.  `env` at level 4 therefore adds NOTHING where the two
coincide, and refines the other 81.4 % of a 1.075 m prism -- the 0.6 m
downstream tail and the 0.125 m upstream run -- for nothing.

  env refinement band, ~4 cells deep, over 0.9425 m2:
      level 1 (9.0000 mm)      46,542 cells
      level 4 (1.1250 mm)   2,978,695 cells      <-- what actually ran

THE COUPLING.  `write_snappy_dict` has no `env` level of its own: it writes
`lev["duct"]` at :1069 for the feature and at :1076-1081 for the surface and its
three named regions.  `reg_surface_levels` returns only {duct, hub, strut} --
THE REGISTRATION REGISTERS NO ENVELOPE LEVEL AT ALL, and `lev["duct"]` was an
un-flagged choice by the builder.  It is invisible while duct is level 1,
because then env is level 1 too and looks deliberate.  It only bites when
somebody raises the duct to clear GEO-8, which is exactly what happened.

