WHERE THIS IS WRONG, NAMED BY ITS AUTHOR.

1. IT IS SILENT WHEN IT CHANGES NOTHING, WHICH IS THE DANGEROUS DIRECTION.  On
   the REGISTERED ladder duct is level 1, so `EL == lev["duct"] == 1` and the
   dict is byte-identical to today's.  The diff can therefore be applied,
   selftested and reviewed WITHOUT EVER EXERCISING THE BRANCH IT EXISTS FOR.
   It must be driven with a throwaway duct override, and the control is the
   emitted dict: `env { level (1 1) }` while `duct { level (4 4) }`.  A test
   that only runs the registered ladder proves nothing here.

2. IT PINS TO THE REGISTERED LEVEL, NOT TO A CHOSEN ONE.  That is deliberate --
   an argv must not set an unregistered quantity -- but it means the envelope's
   resolution is decided by a row that was written to describe the DUCT BORE.
   If a successor registration ever raises the registered duct level for a real
   reason, the envelope silently rises with it again and this defect returns in
   the same shape.  The durable fix is a REGISTERED envelope level; this diff
   does not create one, because inventing a registered quantity in an
   instrument is the defect T26 section 21.7 already recorded.

3. IT DOES NOT PROVE THE INTERFACE STAYS RESOLVED -- it argues it, from
   coincident geometry.  The argument rests on `duct`'s inner bore and
   `env_wall` being the same 96-gon at the same radius and phase, which is
   MEASURED above and phase-asserted by the builder. But snappy resolves
   COINCIDENT surfaces by its own rules, and if it were ever to drop the
   interface, a downstream `checkMesh` region-count or connectivity refusal is
   what would catch it -- not this diff.  THE FIRST BUILD AFTER THIS CHANGE MUST
   HAVE ITS `duct` AND `fluid` CELL COUNTS AND ITS FACE-ZONE COUNT READ, not
   assumed.

4. IT LOWERS THE INLET AND OUTLET CAP RESOLUTION TOO.  `EL` applies to
   `env_wall`, `inlet` and `outlet` alike.  On the registered ladder that is
   status quo; under a raised duct it means the inlet and outlet planes are
   meshed at 9.000 mm rather than 1.125 mm.  That is almost certainly right --
   they are far-field boundaries, not named features -- but it is a change in
   the inlet plane's resolution and it is stated rather than buried.

COST: none.  It removes work.

WITHDRAWN BY THIS DOCUMENT: this lane's earlier statement that `env_wall` must
be split axially in the surface synthesiser.  That was reasoned from the
assumption that `fluid_env` was the only carrier of the fluid/duct interface.
The duct STL carries it, the assumption was wrong, and no synthesiser change is
needed.
